    "kind": "news",
    "check": "every name and figure read from the page image"
  },
  "note": "Poll on Wednesday 21 May 1952, \"the first urban election for at least thirty years\" in Tandragee (Portadown News). Twelve candidates for nine seats; \"no politics entered into the election\". Just over 600 electors on the register, 548 votes cast, 10 spoilt; the paper calls it an 85 per cent poll. William Barr, chairman for 18 years, was defeated. Returning officer J. Sterling. Joseph Cullen was the son of J. R. Cullen, \"the only Nationalist on the old Council\"."
